name: proving this machine
boss: Void
flavor: Nothing you deleted is as gone as you think.
brief: Everything so far was about using a machine. This is about interrogating
brief: one -- and the machine you know least about is the one you are standing
brief: on right now.
brief:
brief: xos makes a specific claim: every block of the root filesystem is
brief: checked against a hash tree whose root hash arrives on the kernel
brief: command line, inside an image signed with keys you enrolled yourself.
brief: You are about to check that claim by hand, with commands you already
brief: know. No special tool. Nothing you could not do on someone else's box.
---
q: {what command line was this kernel booted with|report the kernel's boot command line|which command line did this kernel boot with}
a: cat /proc/cmdline
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: cmdline is the exact string the bootloader handed the kernel
---
q: {pull just the verity root hash out of that command line|extract just the verity root hash from the kernel command line|isolate the sha256 root hash out of /proc/cmdline}
a: sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: it is the 64 hex characters that follow the word sha256
---
q: {what filesystem is mounted at / and with what flags|report the filesystem type and mount flags for /|what type is / mounted as, and with which flags}
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $3, $4}' /proc/mounts
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: awk's $2 and $4 pick the mountpoint and options columns out of /proc/mounts
---
q: {confirm the root is mounted read-only|check that / is mounted read-only|prove the root filesystem is mounted read-only}
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts | grep -o '^ro'
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: grep -o on the flags column proves ro is actually set, not just present nearby
---
q: {what device is the root actually on|which device is / actually mounted from|report the real device backing /}
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $1}' /proc/mounts
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $1 in /proc/mounts is the device column
---
q: {hash the bytes of the filesystem you are standing on|compute the sha256 of the filesystem you are running on|hash the raw bytes of the mounted root filesystem}
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
hint: dm-0 is the device dm-verity hands you once it is checking every block
---
q: %show% just that digest, with the device name cut off
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
teach: cut -f
hint: cut -d' ' -f1 grabs just the hash, dropping the filename sha256sum prints after it
---
q: {confirm the kernel was told to panic rather than continue on a bad block|check that the kernel is set to panic, not continue, on a bad block|prove the kernel will panic instead of carrying on after a bad block}
a: grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: fail closed. a verified system that carries on after a mismatch is not one
---
q: {what does the kernel say about verity in its boot log|report what the kernel logged about verity while booting|find what the kernel's boot log says about verity}
a: dmesg | grep -i verity
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg
hint: dmesg's boot log names the verity target as it starts checking blocks
---
q: {is the kernel locked down, and how far|report the kernel's current lockdown level|check whether the kernel is locked down, and to what degree}
a: cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: lockdown has stages; cat just reads the current one
---
q: {prove there is no module loader to insert code into this kernel|show that this kernel has no module loader to insert code through|confirm no module loader exists on this kernel}
a: [ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo absent
ref: [
teach: [ -e
hint: -e tests for existence; no /proc/modules means no module loader at all
---
q: {prove /dev/mem is not there to read or write physical memory through|show that /dev/mem does not exist on this system|confirm there is no /dev/mem to read or write physical memory through}
a: [ -e /dev/mem ] && echo present || echo absent
ref: [
teach: [ -e
hint: -e tests for existence, same idea as the modules check above
---
q: {prove the root filesystem refuses to be remounted writable|show that attempting to remount / read-write is refused|confirm / cannot be remounted writable}
a: mount -o remount,rw / 2>/dev/null && echo WRITABLE || echo refused
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
hint: remount,rw attempts the write; the kernel refuses it if verity is enforced
---
q: {prove nothing dropped in your home directory can be executed|show that a binary copied into your home directory cannot run|confirm code placed in $HOME cannot be executed}
a: cp /bin/busybox $HOME/x && chmod +x $HOME/x && $HOME/x true && echo ran || echo refused
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: $HOME is on a tmpfs mounted noexec
---
q: %count% how many trust anchors are compiled into the tls binary
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: strings pulls printable text out of the binary; grep -c counts the matches
---
q: %list% every command this system documents, and count them
a: learn list | wc -l
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: learn
teach: learn
hint: learn list enumerates every documented command; wc -l counts the lines
